WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.831 --> 00:00:02.421
- Hi everyone, Sal Khan here.

00:00:02.421 --> 00:00:05.051
Welcome to this Homeroom live stream,

00:00:05.051 --> 00:00:07.171
as always, I'm very excited
about the conversation

00:00:07.171 --> 00:00:08.911
we're going to have with our guest today,

00:00:08.911 --> 00:00:10.961
Representative Karen Bass.

00:00:10.961 --> 00:00:11.951
But before we get to that,

00:00:11.951 --> 00:00:13.671
I'll give my standard announcements.

00:00:13.671 --> 00:00:15.801
First of all, a reminder that Khan Academy

00:00:15.801 --> 00:00:18.531
is a not-for-profit
organization, we only exist

00:00:18.531 --> 00:00:21.211
through philanthropic donations
from folks like yourself,

00:00:21.211 --> 00:00:22.231
if you're in a position to do so.

00:00:22.231 --> 00:00:24.431
So if you can, please think about going

00:00:24.431 --> 00:00:27.411
to Khanacademy.org/donate.

00:00:27.411 --> 00:00:29.241
I also wanna give a special shout out

00:00:29.241 --> 00:00:31.901
to several organizations
that have been helping

00:00:31.901 --> 00:00:33.881
to support Khan Academy,
especially as we've gone

00:00:33.881 --> 00:00:35.621
through the COVID crisis.

00:00:35.621 --> 00:00:38.571
We were already running at
a deficit before the crisis,

00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:40.831
and you could imagine
the load on our servers

00:00:40.831 --> 00:00:43.521
has been a two and a
half, three times normal,

00:00:43.521 --> 00:00:45.951
registrations have been
more than 10 times normal,

00:00:45.951 --> 00:00:48.761
we've been trying to accelerate
a whole series of content

00:00:48.761 --> 00:00:51.521
and product and programmatic elements,

00:00:51.521 --> 00:00:53.931
and to help us there, special
thanks to Bank of America,

00:00:53.931 --> 00:00:56.241
Google.org, AT&amp;T, Fastly, Novartis,

00:00:56.241 --> 00:00:57.801
and actually the Amgen Foundation

00:00:57.801 --> 00:01:00.141
and the many other supporters
of Khan Academy over the years

00:01:00.141 --> 00:01:02.571
that have really put us in
a position to be able to do

00:01:02.571 --> 00:01:04.871
the work that we're able
to do now to support

00:01:04.871 --> 00:01:07.831
so many folks through this
difficult time period.

00:01:07.831 --> 00:01:11.751
I also wanna remind folks that
if you've enjoyed watching

00:01:11.751 --> 00:01:14.261
these Homeroom live streams,
or maybe you haven't had

00:01:14.261 --> 00:01:15.791
a chance to watch as many,
and you would just like

00:01:15.791 --> 00:01:19.411
to listen to some of
them, this live stream

00:01:19.411 --> 00:01:22.071
is adapted for podcasts now,

00:01:22.071 --> 00:01:24.895
and so you can find Homeroom
with Sal the Podcast,

00:01:24.895 --> 00:01:29.242
anywhere that you would
typically find your podcasts.

00:01:29.242 --> 00:01:31.201
So with that, I'm excited

00:01:31.201 --> 00:01:34.839
to introduce Representative Karen Bass,

00:01:34.839 --> 00:01:38.035
member of the US House of Representatives

00:01:38.035 --> 00:01:42.561
representing the 33rd
district of California,

00:01:42.561 --> 00:01:45.531
former speaker of the California Assembly,

00:01:45.531 --> 00:01:49.011
chair of the House Black Caucus.

00:01:49.011 --> 00:01:51.561
Thanks for joining us Representative Bass.

00:01:51.561 --> 00:01:54.211
- Thanks for having me on,
nice to be with you today.

00:01:55.351 --> 00:01:56.671
- So there's a bunch of stuff,

00:01:56.671 --> 00:01:59.666
I'm super eager to cover with you.

00:01:59.666 --> 00:02:02.201
First of all, your life's journey

00:02:02.201 --> 00:02:03.971
I think is very inspirational,
so I wanna go into that.

00:02:03.971 --> 00:02:05.921
A lot of young people kind
of trying to figure out

00:02:05.921 --> 00:02:06.754
what to do with life.

00:02:06.754 --> 00:02:08.371
I think the second element is,

00:02:08.371 --> 00:02:10.921
where it's some major
milestones in history

00:02:10.921 --> 00:02:12.861
or at least anniversaries of milestones,

00:02:12.861 --> 00:02:15.661
we've the ratification
of the 19th amendment,

00:02:15.661 --> 00:02:20.234
as well as Martin Luther King's
March on Washington in 1963,

00:02:20.234 --> 00:02:22.681
kind of the 57th anniversary of that.

00:02:22.681 --> 00:02:24.751
And I'm also just curious about politics

00:02:24.751 --> 00:02:27.351
and the operations of Congress generally.

00:02:27.351 --> 00:02:30.000
But maybe what we'll
take it in that order.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:30.833
- Sure.

00:02:30.833 --> 00:02:32.761
- For young people watching,

00:02:32.761 --> 00:02:34.111
and I don't think we get enough of this

00:02:34.111 --> 00:02:36.151
when we watched the mainstream press,

00:02:36.151 --> 00:02:38.021
they'll see folks like yourself,

00:02:38.021 --> 00:02:40.301
who I've looked like
they've accomplished a lot

00:02:40.301 --> 00:02:41.491
and have accomplished a lot.

00:02:41.491 --> 00:02:43.371
And they're like, "How do I do that?

00:02:43.371 --> 00:02:45.981
What are the steps that
I take in order to become

00:02:45.981 --> 00:02:47.841
the next Karen Bass?"
(Karen laughing)

00:02:47.841 --> 00:02:49.361
So as much as you can tell us about

00:02:49.361 --> 00:02:50.421
what was your own journey,

00:02:50.421 --> 00:02:53.451
maybe starting in middle and high school?

00:02:53.451 --> 00:02:55.866
- Sure, well, first of
all, I will tell you that,

00:02:55.866 --> 00:02:58.451
my journey, I never imagined

00:02:58.451 --> 00:03:01.451
that I would be a member of
Congress, that's most important,

00:03:01.451 --> 00:03:05.031
but I did decide at a very
young age that I really wanted

00:03:05.031 --> 00:03:08.041
to focus on improving the quality of life

00:03:08.041 --> 00:03:10.611
and addressing social and economic issues.

00:03:10.611 --> 00:03:13.491
And that's because I
grew up in a time period

00:03:13.491 --> 00:03:17.981
when all of that was happening,
post-civil rights, antiwar,

00:03:17.981 --> 00:03:20.421
everything was exploding in the world,

00:03:20.421 --> 00:03:22.971
and I was very captured by that.

00:03:22.971 --> 00:03:25.851
And so it didn't occur to me

00:03:25.851 --> 00:03:28.631
that I could actually make a living

00:03:28.631 --> 00:03:30.221
and be politically involved.

00:03:30.221 --> 00:03:32.801
And so I whipped down many paths,

00:03:32.801 --> 00:03:34.731
one of which was the medical field.

00:03:34.731 --> 00:03:37.401
So I worked in the medical
field, one is a nurse,

00:03:37.401 --> 00:03:40.311
and then as a physician
assistant for a number of years,

00:03:40.311 --> 00:03:43.301
and after work, I
volunteered in the community

00:03:43.301 --> 00:03:45.081
and focused on different issues.

00:03:45.081 --> 00:03:47.332
Now, ironically, given
what's happening today,

00:03:47.332 --> 00:03:51.681
one of the first issues I
focused on was police abuse,

00:03:51.681 --> 00:03:54.811
because we had terrible
problems in Los Angeles,

00:03:54.811 --> 00:03:57.511
and we are on the heels within 24 hours

00:03:57.511 --> 00:03:59.341
of two terrible shootings

00:03:59.341 --> 00:04:01.261
that took place in two different states.

00:04:01.261 --> 00:04:05.781
So many decades later the
issues are still the same.

00:04:05.781 --> 00:04:09.311
And after a while I started
a community organization

00:04:09.311 --> 00:04:11.821
that was also focused on these issues,

00:04:11.821 --> 00:04:13.351
and from there ran for office.

00:04:13.351 --> 00:04:15.508
But I did not set out to say,

00:04:15.508 --> 00:04:17.248
"I wanna be an elected official."

00:04:18.581 --> 00:04:20.821
- And to any young people out there.

00:04:20.821 --> 00:04:22.971
I think there's kind of two
categories of young people,

00:04:22.971 --> 00:04:25.738
there's those who are
idealistic, and they say,

00:04:25.738 --> 00:04:27.051
"I could run for office

00:04:27.051 --> 00:04:28.951
and I could play a role in civic change."

00:04:28.951 --> 00:04:31.151
And it could be as being
an elected official,

00:04:31.151 --> 00:04:32.761
it could be in other ways.

00:04:32.761 --> 00:04:35.041
But there's also increasing cynicism

00:04:35.041 --> 00:04:37.921
about politics and polarization,

00:04:37.921 --> 00:04:39.011
and I wanna talk more about that.

00:04:39.011 --> 00:04:40.901
But just from your point of view,

00:04:40.901 --> 00:04:43.001
what would you tell a young person?

00:04:43.001 --> 00:04:44.741
Who's right there, are they both right?

00:04:44.741 --> 00:04:47.161
Is there a way to have
a more idealistic track

00:04:47.161 --> 00:04:49.030
in politics and policy?

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:51.451
- Well, I think they both are right.

00:04:51.451 --> 00:04:55.541
And I went through periods
where I felt cynical as well,

00:04:55.541 --> 00:04:57.261
but cynical about what?

00:04:57.261 --> 00:05:00.011
I've never felt cynical about people

00:05:00.011 --> 00:05:02.911
and people's ability
to bring about change.

00:05:02.911 --> 00:05:06.021
But one thing about politics
is that there's so many ways

00:05:06.021 --> 00:05:09.121
to get involved in being an
office is just one of them.

00:05:09.121 --> 00:05:10.891
And so when I went through a period

00:05:10.891 --> 00:05:12.521
where I was pretty cynical,

00:05:12.521 --> 00:05:14.541
I was involved on a community level,

00:05:14.541 --> 00:05:16.941
and I wasn't interested
in elected politics.

00:05:16.941 --> 00:05:21.111
That's why I said I never
imagined myself being in Congress.

00:05:21.111 --> 00:05:23.001
But I think at a certain point in time,

00:05:23.001 --> 00:05:25.371
if you're really committed to change,

00:05:25.371 --> 00:05:27.378
then you wanna see something happen.

00:05:27.378 --> 00:05:30.751
And so getting involved
in a community level,

00:05:30.751 --> 00:05:33.181
I did that first, and then I was working

00:05:33.181 --> 00:05:35.788
with elected officials
to bring about change,

00:05:35.788 --> 00:05:40.361
and then ultimately decided
to go in on that path myself.

00:05:40.361 --> 00:05:43.501
But let me just say
that if you are cynical

00:05:43.501 --> 00:05:47.231
and if you make the choice
to not be involved at all,

00:05:47.231 --> 00:05:50.331
then you are essentially
allowing other people

00:05:50.331 --> 00:05:52.171
to control your life,

00:05:52.171 --> 00:05:54.841
and I think that's an
important decision to make.

00:05:54.841 --> 00:05:57.021
Do you want to be in the driver's seat

00:05:57.021 --> 00:05:59.621
or do you wanna be in the passenger seat?

00:05:59.621 --> 00:06:04.081
And regardless of whether you
choose to be involved or not,

00:06:04.081 --> 00:06:07.931
the powers that be will
be involved in your life.

00:06:07.931 --> 00:06:09.581
So it's a question of whether or not

00:06:09.581 --> 00:06:11.864
you wanna participate with them or not.

00:06:13.031 --> 00:06:14.871
- And what advice would
you have for young people

00:06:14.871 --> 00:06:16.061
who wanna be in that driver's seat,

00:06:16.061 --> 00:06:17.691
or at least start exercising that muscle

00:06:17.691 --> 00:06:19.766
of nudging their way
into the driver's seat?

00:06:19.766 --> 00:06:22.264
What could what could they
start doing right now?

00:06:23.141 --> 00:06:25.381
- Right now, you see the wonderful thing

00:06:25.381 --> 00:06:28.691
about the generation of young
people that are watching this,

00:06:28.691 --> 00:06:32.081
is that you have technology
that didn't always exist,

00:06:32.081 --> 00:06:35.551
and so there's so many
ways to get involved now,

00:06:35.551 --> 00:06:37.162
there's tons of organizations

00:06:37.162 --> 00:06:39.861
that focus on different issues.

00:06:39.861 --> 00:06:42.051
So what I always tell people is that,

00:06:42.051 --> 00:06:43.291
where's your heart?

00:06:43.291 --> 00:06:45.371
What really interests you?

00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:46.681
Is it the environment?

00:06:46.681 --> 00:06:48.001
Is it immigration?

00:06:48.001 --> 00:06:49.120
Is race?

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:51.121
All so many different issues.

00:06:51.121 --> 00:06:53.921
Find out an issue that interests you,

00:06:53.921 --> 00:06:55.891
and then find out other people

00:06:55.891 --> 00:06:57.361
that are involved in that issue,

00:06:57.361 --> 00:07:01.761
because now with technology,
any issue you pick

00:07:01.761 --> 00:07:04.174
there's millions of
people already involved.

00:07:05.371 --> 00:07:08.081
- Absolutely, and I wanna
remind everyone watching,

00:07:08.081 --> 00:07:10.371
you can put your questions
in the message boards

00:07:10.371 --> 00:07:12.251
on YouTube and Facebook,
and we have team members

00:07:12.251 --> 00:07:14.981
who will surface those
questions to Representative Bass

00:07:14.981 --> 00:07:16.571
and to myself, and I'll try to get to

00:07:16.571 --> 00:07:18.961
as many of those as, as possible.

00:07:18.961 --> 00:07:20.561
- Can I add one other thing.

00:07:20.561 --> 00:07:22.991
Is that it doesn't
matter what age you are.

00:07:22.991 --> 00:07:25.601
I have worked with eight
year olds and nine year olds

00:07:25.601 --> 00:07:27.231
who were interested in politics,

00:07:27.231 --> 00:07:29.720
now today they're 20 year olds.

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:30.731
(Karen laughing)

00:07:30.731 --> 00:07:33.691
So your age should not prohibit you

00:07:33.691 --> 00:07:37.879
from being involved at all,
don't see that as a barrier.

00:07:37.879 --> 00:07:39.741
- You know when you say
that, it reminds me,

00:07:39.741 --> 00:07:42.451
I remember when I was, let's
call it 12 or 13 years old,

00:07:42.451 --> 00:07:44.461
and you're pondering your
place in the universe.

00:07:44.461 --> 00:07:46.101
And I always looked at people

00:07:46.101 --> 00:07:48.161
who are 30, 40, 50, 60
years old, and I'm like,

00:07:48.161 --> 00:07:50.481
they must have it all figured
out, they've been around

00:07:50.481 --> 00:07:52.181
for so long.
- (laughs) right.

00:07:52.181 --> 00:07:53.601
- The reality is adults
stop thinking about

00:07:53.601 --> 00:07:55.631
a lot of this stuff,
a lot of kids actually

00:07:55.631 --> 00:07:59.081
are deeper thinkers about
society than a lot of adults.

00:07:59.081 --> 00:08:02.651
So kids don't feel insecure about that,

00:08:02.651 --> 00:08:04.641
you have as much of a
right to have an opinion

00:08:04.641 --> 00:08:06.741
about these things as anybody.

00:08:06.741 --> 00:08:09.161
Given these anniversaries
we're going into,

00:08:09.161 --> 00:08:11.001
which is always a time for reflection,

00:08:11.001 --> 00:08:14.121
you have the hundred year
anniversary of the certification,

00:08:14.121 --> 00:08:16.641
I guess the ratification
of the 19th Amendment,

00:08:16.641 --> 00:08:20.621
giving women the right to
vote, 57th year anniversary of,

00:08:20.621 --> 00:08:24.541
or about two on August 28 hit,
of the March on Washington.

00:08:24.541 --> 00:08:26.391
You know, these are
related, you could argue,

00:08:26.391 --> 00:08:28.031
but the women's right to vote

00:08:28.031 --> 00:08:30.551
and participate in politics
is a civil rights issue,

00:08:30.551 --> 00:08:31.791
and then you have obviously
the broader notion

00:08:31.791 --> 00:08:33.481
of civil rights when it comes to race,

00:08:33.481 --> 00:08:35.244
and these feel very connected.

00:08:36.191 --> 00:08:38.931
Where do you think, and
you can take them separate

00:08:38.931 --> 00:08:41.691
or intermingled these
two, where do you think

00:08:41.691 --> 00:08:45.961
we are on that journey
in both women's rights

00:08:45.961 --> 00:08:47.951
and civil rights in general?

00:08:47.951 --> 00:08:49.381
- Well, let me just say, first of all,

00:08:49.381 --> 00:08:52.241
that it is the 100th
anniversary, but it really

00:08:52.241 --> 00:08:54.471
that when the 19th Amendment was signed,

00:08:54.471 --> 00:08:56.281
it did not apply to all women,

00:08:56.281 --> 00:08:58.021
so it only applied to white women.

00:08:58.021 --> 00:09:00.541
Black women were not
allowed the right to vote,

00:09:00.541 --> 00:09:04.881
neither were indigenous women
or women who were immigrants.

00:09:04.881 --> 00:09:08.751
And so, um, it is an
anniversary to celebrate,

00:09:08.751 --> 00:09:12.885
but I think it's an example
of how we have a rocky road.

00:09:12.885 --> 00:09:15.731
I mean, we move forward,
there's some progress,

00:09:15.731 --> 00:09:18.441
and then sometimes we have a move back.

00:09:18.441 --> 00:09:21.091
So while we're celebrating
these anniversaries,

00:09:21.091 --> 00:09:24.551
we are 69 days away
from the next election,

00:09:24.551 --> 00:09:28.981
and everybody watching this
who is 18 years old and older,

00:09:28.981 --> 00:09:30.981
I know you're registered to vote

00:09:30.981 --> 00:09:32.664
and I know you plan to vote.

00:09:33.731 --> 00:09:38.731
Even in 2020, we still
have to deal with the fact

00:09:39.331 --> 00:09:41.131
that people in the United States

00:09:41.131 --> 00:09:43.911
do not automatically
have the right to vote.

00:09:43.911 --> 00:09:46.051
Because now there are tactics

00:09:46.051 --> 00:09:48.391
that are done, voter suppression.

00:09:48.391 --> 00:09:52.211
So the latest area that we
have to be concerned about

00:09:52.211 --> 00:09:54.591
is the post office and COVID.

00:09:54.591 --> 00:09:57.523
So here we have an election 69 days away,

00:09:57.523 --> 00:10:02.031
and people are worried
about going out to vote

00:10:02.031 --> 00:10:04.481
because they might get exposed to COVID,

00:10:04.481 --> 00:10:07.541
and in some States they're
not gonna make it easy

00:10:07.541 --> 00:10:09.601
for people to vote from home.

00:10:09.601 --> 00:10:14.291
And so 2020, we are still
fighting some of the same issues,

00:10:14.291 --> 00:10:17.111
there has been progress,
but one thing we've learned

00:10:17.111 --> 00:10:20.621
from the last few years is
that you can make progress,

00:10:20.621 --> 00:10:22.711
but that doesn't mean it's permanent,

00:10:22.711 --> 00:10:26.041
you always have to be
vigilant and stay on it

00:10:26.041 --> 00:10:29.464
because the progress you
made can be reversed.

00:10:31.041 --> 00:10:33.571
- And from your point of
view, I mean, I'm curious,

00:10:33.571 --> 00:10:36.871
the voter suppression
tactics over the years,

00:10:36.871 --> 00:10:38.261
obviously if you go back decades,

00:10:38.261 --> 00:10:40.861
you had things like poll
tests and things like that.

00:10:42.091 --> 00:10:44.481
Do you think that we're kind
of in some ways reverting,

00:10:44.481 --> 00:10:46.441
I mean, some of this conversation
with the post office,

00:10:46.441 --> 00:10:49.041
I mean, you're much closer
to the issue than I am.

00:10:49.041 --> 00:10:50.681
I mean, what is going on?

00:10:50.681 --> 00:10:53.581
I mean, one view is, does
it hurt everyone equally?

00:10:53.581 --> 00:10:55.011
And so maybe it doesn't,
- No.

00:10:55.011 --> 00:10:56.881
- Have a dramatic outcome.

00:10:56.881 --> 00:10:58.701
I'd love to hear your thoughts.

00:10:58.701 --> 00:11:02.351
- Sure, sadly to say that
voter suppression tactics

00:11:02.351 --> 00:11:04.501
are very targeted and they're targeted

00:11:04.501 --> 00:11:06.911
to communities of color,

00:11:06.911 --> 00:11:09.971
they're also targeted to
young people and to students.

00:11:09.971 --> 00:11:12.801
And so the form that voter suppression

00:11:12.801 --> 00:11:14.891
has taken over the last few years,

00:11:14.891 --> 00:11:17.549
and this is all because the Supreme Court

00:11:17.549 --> 00:11:21.251
did a headache decision a few years ago

00:11:21.251 --> 00:11:23.491
that essentially pulled the teeth

00:11:23.491 --> 00:11:24.961
out of the Voting Rights Act.

00:11:24.961 --> 00:11:26.821
And one of the main accomplishments

00:11:26.821 --> 00:11:29.941
of the civil rights movement
was the Voting Rights Act,

00:11:29.941 --> 00:11:33.181
which protected the
right to vote initially

00:11:33.181 --> 00:11:36.691
for black people, but also
for other people of color,

00:11:36.691 --> 00:11:38.881
where in the South in particular,

00:11:38.881 --> 00:11:41.211
but this also happens around the country,

00:11:41.211 --> 00:11:44.490
but in the South black people
had to risk their lives

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:46.561
to go vote, and sometimes

00:11:46.561 --> 00:11:49.651
they had to suffer violent consequences.

00:11:49.651 --> 00:11:52.311
And so the federal
government had to step in

00:11:52.311 --> 00:11:54.361
to protect the right to vote.

00:11:54.361 --> 00:11:56.201
While the Supreme Court a few years ago

00:11:56.201 --> 00:11:58.231
really kind of undid that.

00:11:58.231 --> 00:12:01.131
And so the minute the Supreme
Court made that decision,

00:12:01.131 --> 00:12:04.521
within hours of that decision being made,

00:12:04.521 --> 00:12:08.971
many States imposed
very strong restrictions

00:12:08.971 --> 00:12:12.204
that limited the right to
vote for certain people.

00:12:13.741 --> 00:12:16.261
- One thing I'm curious,
as a member of Congress

00:12:16.261 --> 00:12:19.671
you obviously interact a lot with people,

00:12:19.671 --> 00:12:20.971
Congress members from throughout

00:12:20.971 --> 00:12:22.491
the country of both parties,

00:12:22.491 --> 00:12:25.751
and actually you have a reputation
of being very well liked

00:12:25.751 --> 00:12:28.621
and respected by both sides of the aisle.

00:12:28.621 --> 00:12:31.815
When you talk to folks
who are on the other side

00:12:31.815 --> 00:12:35.781
and you bring up these
issues, are they kind of like,

00:12:35.781 --> 00:12:36.891
Yeah, this is a real issue,

00:12:36.891 --> 00:12:40.041
but there's all this
political gamesmanship

00:12:40.041 --> 00:12:43.221
to try to make the numbers
work for one side or the other,

00:12:43.221 --> 00:12:45.921
or do you feel that they're
just not seeing the issue?

00:12:47.131 --> 00:12:49.621
- Well, I know my colleagues see

00:12:49.621 --> 00:12:51.652
the issue there, they're smart.

00:12:51.652 --> 00:12:53.011
(Karen laughing)

00:12:53.011 --> 00:12:57.841
The sad thing is that people
in power wanna stay in power,

00:12:57.841 --> 00:12:59.661
they wanna stay in office.

00:12:59.661 --> 00:13:02.071
And so what they tend to say

00:13:02.071 --> 00:13:04.811
is they tend to talk about voter fraud,

00:13:04.811 --> 00:13:07.801
and that they're protecting
against voter fraud.

00:13:07.801 --> 00:13:11.832
The problem is, is that there
is just virtually no evidence

00:13:11.832 --> 00:13:14.961
that voter fraud is a very big problem.

00:13:14.961 --> 00:13:18.871
As a matter of fact, the
most significant voter fraud

00:13:18.871 --> 00:13:22.731
that took place in 2018
took place in North Carolina

00:13:22.731 --> 00:13:24.871
and it was done by Republicans.

00:13:24.871 --> 00:13:26.691
So one thing that you learn about

00:13:26.691 --> 00:13:28.711
when you serve in a legislative body,

00:13:28.711 --> 00:13:31.351
whether that's local state or federal,

00:13:31.351 --> 00:13:33.421
is you learn to compartmentalize.

00:13:33.421 --> 00:13:36.191
So there are some subjects, some issues

00:13:36.191 --> 00:13:39.631
that I work very well with
my Republican colleagues on,

00:13:39.631 --> 00:13:42.041
and then there's some issues
that we don't talk about

00:13:42.041 --> 00:13:45.651
because we know we have
diametrically opposing views.

00:13:45.651 --> 00:13:49.721
But important is is that
whether you agree or disagree,

00:13:49.721 --> 00:13:51.481
there's no reason at all

00:13:51.481 --> 00:13:54.671
not to be respectful and
nice to the other side.

00:13:54.671 --> 00:13:58.681
So I might disagree with
them terribly on one issue,

00:13:58.681 --> 00:14:01.231
but then 10 other issues we agree on.

00:14:01.231 --> 00:14:04.741
And so you tend to
focus where you can work

00:14:04.741 --> 00:14:07.574
and compartmentalize where you can't.

00:14:09.581 --> 00:14:10.751
- It makes a ton of sense,

00:14:10.751 --> 00:14:12.151
and I actually have more
questions about Congress.

00:14:12.151 --> 00:14:13.781
But a really good question
from YouTube came up

00:14:13.781 --> 00:14:16.861
from Rupesh Cathy, says,
"Representative Bass,

00:14:16.861 --> 00:14:19.881
the racial discrimination
has been such a main issue

00:14:19.881 --> 00:14:21.701
in recent years, but it
seems like we're seeing

00:14:21.701 --> 00:14:23.760
the same results over and over.

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:25.858
How do we really improve it?"

00:14:27.281 --> 00:14:31.261
- Well, actually we have suffered terribly

00:14:31.261 --> 00:14:33.556
over these last three and a half years,

00:14:33.556 --> 00:14:38.241
because we have someone at
the top who's pretty open

00:14:38.241 --> 00:14:41.631
about his feelings about other people.

00:14:41.631 --> 00:14:45.471
But at the same time, because he has been

00:14:47.910 --> 00:14:48.743
(Karen laughing)

00:14:48.743 --> 00:14:52.241
so divisive, let me
say, it's allowed people

00:14:52.241 --> 00:14:57.241
to see race in another way, and
I'm actually very encouraged

00:14:58.050 --> 00:15:02.011
after the horrible murder of George Floyd,

00:15:02.011 --> 00:15:06.071
that there is a new awareness
in our country about racism.

00:15:06.071 --> 00:15:08.211
What we have tended to do in our country

00:15:08.211 --> 00:15:11.031
is we only like the good
part of our history,

00:15:11.031 --> 00:15:14.071
we don't wanna hear the
bad news, and so it's hard

00:15:14.071 --> 00:15:16.561
for people to accept that slavery existed

00:15:16.561 --> 00:15:19.421
in this United States for over 200 years,

00:15:19.421 --> 00:15:21.451
250 years to be exact,

00:15:21.451 --> 00:15:25.431
100 years before the
country was actually formed.

00:15:25.431 --> 00:15:27.481
And so we don't wanna talk

00:15:27.481 --> 00:15:30.041
about these difficult subjects like race.

00:15:30.041 --> 00:15:33.161
But I feel like, especially
with the younger generation,

00:15:33.161 --> 00:15:37.371
there is an openness now to
say there is discrimination

00:15:37.371 --> 00:15:40.411
within the institutions of our society,

00:15:40.411 --> 00:15:43.381
let's examine where the
discrimination exists

00:15:43.381 --> 00:15:44.731
and let's get rid of it.

00:15:44.731 --> 00:15:49.111
And I'm hopeful that
we're about to approach

00:15:49.111 --> 00:15:52.971
a new milestone in our
history, but we are going

00:15:52.971 --> 00:15:55.491
to have to have new leadership at the top

00:15:55.491 --> 00:15:57.234
in order for that to take place.

00:15:58.161 --> 00:16:00.541
- And where do you think
the rubber hits the road?

00:16:00.541 --> 00:16:03.722
Clearly what happens at the
national level sets a tone,

00:16:03.722 --> 00:16:08.211
kind of sets a context in
which everything happens.

00:16:08.211 --> 00:16:11.181
But when we talk, think
about George Floyd's murder,

00:16:11.181 --> 00:16:13.481
that seems like a local
police reform issue,

00:16:13.481 --> 00:16:15.381
although it seems like
it's somewhat systemic

00:16:15.381 --> 00:16:18.351
across many geographies.

00:16:18.351 --> 00:16:21.371
What levers do you have in
Congress or at the federal level?

00:16:21.371 --> 00:16:22.831
And then what are the other levels?

00:16:22.831 --> 00:16:24.051
Is it mayors?

00:16:24.051 --> 00:16:25.291
Is it police commissioners?

00:16:25.291 --> 00:16:28.151
Or is it something else
that changes things?

00:16:28.151 --> 00:16:30.371
- Sure, it is all of the above,

00:16:30.371 --> 00:16:33.501
and I would encourage your listeners

00:16:33.501 --> 00:16:37.821
to watch a documentary on
Netflix that's called "13th."

00:16:37.821 --> 00:16:39.951
It's about the 13th Amendment.

00:16:39.951 --> 00:16:42.451
Watch that documentary
because it goes through

00:16:42.451 --> 00:16:45.251
the history of policing
in the United States.

00:16:45.251 --> 00:16:48.211
So this has long been an issue,

00:16:48.211 --> 00:16:50.495
especially with African Americans.

00:16:50.495 --> 00:16:54.710
And I think that, uh, every
level of government is involved.

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:58.191
So the rubber meets the
road on the federal level,

00:16:58.191 --> 00:17:00.661
because the federal government
has the purse strings.

00:17:00.661 --> 00:17:04.281
We control the money, which
is given out on a local level.

00:17:04.281 --> 00:17:05.731
The locals are important

00:17:05.731 --> 00:17:08.491
because they implement and set policy.

00:17:08.491 --> 00:17:10.771
So after George Floyd's murder,

00:17:10.771 --> 00:17:13.371
we pass legislation in the house.

00:17:13.371 --> 00:17:15.361
Now it stalled in the Senate.

00:17:15.361 --> 00:17:18.911
But the good news is,
just the mere fact of us

00:17:18.911 --> 00:17:23.911
going about introducing
legislation, led to many states

00:17:24.491 --> 00:17:29.091
and cities and counties, also
moving ahead in advance of us

00:17:29.091 --> 00:17:31.371
and passing their own legislation

00:17:31.371 --> 00:17:33.911
to outlaw things like choke-holds,

00:17:33.911 --> 00:17:36.951
where a police officer can
choke somebody to death,

00:17:36.951 --> 00:17:40.771
or a no-knock, which Brianna Taylor died

00:17:40.771 --> 00:17:44.071
when her door was smashed in,

00:17:44.071 --> 00:17:46.081
and the officers were in the wrong house.

00:17:46.081 --> 00:17:48.871
Now it's unveiled that
they've done that before,

00:17:48.871 --> 00:17:51.825
and they shot her, here
she's in bed asleep.

00:17:51.825 --> 00:17:56.041
So there are certain
practices that police have

00:17:56.041 --> 00:17:59.041
that make it very difficult
to sue police officers,

00:17:59.041 --> 00:18:01.671
to fire them or to prosecute them.

00:18:01.671 --> 00:18:04.641
That's why the man that
was killing George Floyd

00:18:04.641 --> 00:18:07.141
on video was looking dead at the camera,

00:18:07.141 --> 00:18:09.721
he wasn't worried because he knew

00:18:09.721 --> 00:18:11.891
he could act with complete impunity.

00:18:11.891 --> 00:18:13.711
We need to change those laws,

00:18:13.711 --> 00:18:17.014
so officers cannot act
with complete impunity.

00:18:18.551 --> 00:18:22.121
- And that's just one dimension of kind of

00:18:23.510 --> 00:18:25.416
this ongoing movement
towards racial equality,

00:18:25.416 --> 00:18:27.971
and we also talk about gender equality.

00:18:27.971 --> 00:18:30.851
What are, if you could wave a magic wand

00:18:30.851 --> 00:18:32.311
from a legislative point of view

00:18:32.311 --> 00:18:34.821
or our other points of
view, what are the things

00:18:34.821 --> 00:18:36.481
that we should be striving for,

00:18:36.481 --> 00:18:39.621
we should be working for, forward towards?

00:18:39.621 --> 00:18:43.651
- Well, first of all, in
the environment of COVID,

00:18:43.651 --> 00:18:46.601
I am extremely concerned about

00:18:46.601 --> 00:18:50.871
the disproportionate death rate
amongst communities of color

00:18:50.871 --> 00:18:53.441
and, and healthcare, that's one.

00:18:53.441 --> 00:18:56.931
I'm also because of COVID very concerned

00:18:56.931 --> 00:18:59.444
about education and the achievement gap,

00:19:00.584 --> 00:19:02.141
a great role of Khan Academy complaint.

00:19:02.141 --> 00:19:05.771
Is that many households do not necessarily

00:19:05.771 --> 00:19:09.081
have the technology or
the parents aren't there,

00:19:09.081 --> 00:19:10.851
especially with young children

00:19:10.851 --> 00:19:12.641
because they're essential workers.

00:19:12.641 --> 00:19:15.001
We already have an achievement gap

00:19:16.283 --> 00:19:19.571
between kids of color and white children.

00:19:19.571 --> 00:19:22.551
Well now, with COVID, I'm concerned that

00:19:22.551 --> 00:19:26.441
that achievement gap is
going to be really increased,

00:19:26.441 --> 00:19:28.361
and so we need to think about that

00:19:28.361 --> 00:19:30.431
when we're past this pandemic.

00:19:30.431 --> 00:19:33.001
And then of course the entire
criminal justice system,

00:19:33.001 --> 00:19:35.641
which involves policing on one end,

00:19:35.641 --> 00:19:39.091
is that we have more
prisoners in the United States

00:19:39.091 --> 00:19:42.504
by proportion than any
other country in the world.

00:19:44.931 --> 00:19:46.361
There's just no reason for
that, especially because

00:19:47.229 --> 00:19:48.171
the vast majority of people
that are incarcerated

00:19:48.171 --> 00:19:51.021
are poor people who can't afford bail,

00:19:51.021 --> 00:19:53.931
and that is one of the big
reasons why they're locked up,

00:19:53.931 --> 00:19:56.044
this just shouldn't exist in our country.

00:19:57.411 --> 00:19:59.331
- Absolutely, I mean, there's
these three dimensions,

00:19:59.331 --> 00:20:01.541
there's kind of the
criminal justice system,

00:20:01.541 --> 00:20:03.271
there's education that you just mentioned,

00:20:03.271 --> 00:20:04.781
and obviously that's where

00:20:04.781 --> 00:20:06.231
we are trying our best, you're right,

00:20:06.231 --> 00:20:09.191
we're part of a much bigger ecosystem.

00:20:09.191 --> 00:20:11.181
And then there's the economy,\.

00:20:11.181 --> 00:20:13.321
Where are we right when COVID hit,

00:20:13.321 --> 00:20:17.851
and we had the shutdowns
of some form in society,

00:20:17.851 --> 00:20:20.541
Congress, and I would say,
and the executive branch,

00:20:20.541 --> 00:20:23.251
everyone was fairly fast to pass

00:20:23.251 --> 00:20:26.601
these first few stimulus bills.

00:20:26.601 --> 00:20:28.941
Now it seems like things
are a little bit more stuck

00:20:28.941 --> 00:20:30.511
maybe cause we're getting
close to the election.

00:20:30.511 --> 00:20:32.441
What do you think is the right thing

00:20:32.441 --> 00:20:35.211
that needs to happen given
the moment that we're in?

00:20:35.211 --> 00:20:37.741
- And actually because
we're near an election,

00:20:37.741 --> 00:20:40.491
you would think that they
would be fast forward,

00:20:40.491 --> 00:20:44.631
the right thing to do always
is to protect people, period.

00:20:44.631 --> 00:20:47.411
And so the idea that we passed the bill

00:20:47.411 --> 00:20:50.821
over a hundred days ago, that's
languishing in the Senate.

00:20:50.821 --> 00:20:54.571
People lost their unemployment
insurance several weeks ago.

00:20:54.571 --> 00:20:57.622
People need stimulus checks.

00:20:57.622 --> 00:20:59.721
One of the things holding up the bill

00:20:59.721 --> 00:21:01.811
is that some of my colleagues feel,

00:21:01.811 --> 00:21:05.471
if we give a $600 check a week to people

00:21:05.471 --> 00:21:08.841
that that will be a disincentive
for them to go to work.

00:21:08.841 --> 00:21:12.454
That hurts my heart, considering
that we passed legislation

00:21:12.454 --> 00:21:16.391
that gave half a trillion dollars

00:21:16.391 --> 00:21:20.381
to major businesses like
the airlines and the hotels.

00:21:20.381 --> 00:21:22.321
Now I'm fine with that,

00:21:22.321 --> 00:21:25.661
but just like we need to bail them out,

00:21:25.661 --> 00:21:28.931
we need to bail the average
citizen out as well.

00:21:28.931 --> 00:21:33.931
The other thing is, is that
we have 178,000, thousand,

00:21:34.991 --> 00:21:38.031
Americans that died in
the last four months.

00:21:38.031 --> 00:21:40.531
And I don't believe
that they needed to die

00:21:40.531 --> 00:21:44.091
if we had dealt with
the pandemic correctly.

00:21:44.091 --> 00:21:49.091
So we need money for testing,
for tracing and for treatment.

00:21:49.151 --> 00:21:52.001
All of those things are in the bill

00:21:52.001 --> 00:21:54.704
that has been sitting in
the Senate for a 100 days.

00:21:56.171 --> 00:21:58.151
- And I'll add to that, we had Ray Dalio,

00:21:58.151 --> 00:22:00.261
famed hedge fund manager,
runs the largest hedge fund

00:22:00.261 --> 00:22:02.031
in the world, who clearly has

00:22:02.031 --> 00:22:03.561
a very deep understanding of economics.

00:22:03.561 --> 00:22:06.851
And sometimes the left,
right argument is stereotyped

00:22:06.851 --> 00:22:09.231
as the left will say, "Oh,
we need more supports.

00:22:09.231 --> 00:22:10.064
We need to support people."

00:22:10.064 --> 00:22:12.101
And the right would say,
"Well, the incentives

00:22:12.101 --> 00:22:14.451
that might create, or that
might drive inflation."

00:22:14.451 --> 00:22:16.641
But we're in an emergency
where people essentially

00:22:16.641 --> 00:22:19.156
can't work, none of their own choice.

00:22:19.156 --> 00:22:20.340
- Right.

00:22:20.340 --> 00:22:21.511
- And deflation is our biggest risk,

00:22:21.511 --> 00:22:23.361
interest rates are at an all time low.

00:22:23.361 --> 00:22:25.171
And Ray Dalio in his own words who,

00:22:25.171 --> 00:22:26.911
I wouldn't call him a political ideolog

00:22:26.911 --> 00:22:29.308
by any stretch of the
imagination of saying,

00:22:29.308 --> 00:22:30.901
"There's really no better option."

00:22:30.901 --> 00:22:33.781
And this was a conversation
about two months ago.

00:22:33.781 --> 00:22:34.891
- Yeah.
- But clearly,

00:22:34.891 --> 00:22:38.851
we're getting in a place where
the economy is sputtering,

00:22:38.851 --> 00:22:40.421
and the most obvious thing do frankly,

00:22:40.421 --> 00:22:42.131
regardless of what side
of the aisle you're on

00:22:42.131 --> 00:22:45.711
is to just to make sure that
that fuel keeps circulating.

00:22:45.711 --> 00:22:49.520
So just me as my economics
teacher, it makes a lot of sense.

00:22:49.520 --> 00:22:50.353
(Karen laughing)

00:22:50.353 --> 00:22:52.941
- Well, and I don't understand,

00:22:52.941 --> 00:22:55.421
I mean, exactly what you
said, we are near an election.

00:22:55.421 --> 00:22:57.365
So you would think that everyone

00:22:57.365 --> 00:23:00.611
would want to make sure that
communities have support.

00:23:00.611 --> 00:23:03.571
So I am baffled by that,
when I don't understand

00:23:03.571 --> 00:23:05.781
why my colleagues on the
other side of the aisle

00:23:05.781 --> 00:23:08.044
in the Senate are holding up the bill.

00:23:09.181 --> 00:23:11.461
And you know, we've talked about COVID,

00:23:11.461 --> 00:23:13.911
we've talked about kind of the progression

00:23:13.911 --> 00:23:16.321
or a little bit of the
progression on civil rights issue.

00:23:16.321 --> 00:23:19.401
On where women are,
especially women in politics

00:23:19.401 --> 00:23:22.821
or women in society, clearly
there's a lot of progress

00:23:22.821 --> 00:23:25.351
that has been made over
the last hundred years.

00:23:25.351 --> 00:23:26.991
You are in a position of you are

00:23:26.991 --> 00:23:29.321
one of the most influential
Congress people,

00:23:29.321 --> 00:23:30.701
we now have Kamala Harris

00:23:30.701 --> 00:23:34.874
as the vice presidential
nominee on a major ticket.

00:23:36.905 --> 00:23:38.561
Is this the beginning?

00:23:38.561 --> 00:23:40.481
Are we going to get to
a new inflection point?

00:23:40.481 --> 00:23:41.698
Or is this more superficial
and there's still

00:23:41.698 --> 00:23:44.985
some deep seated things
that we need to fix?

00:23:44.985 --> 00:23:46.621
- I think it's also all of the above.

00:23:46.621 --> 00:23:49.256
I mean, I do think that Kamala Harris,

00:23:49.256 --> 00:23:53.451
and hopefully our side will be successful

00:23:53.451 --> 00:23:56.321
and it will be Biden-Harris
sworn in in January,

00:23:56.321 --> 00:24:00.651
that's huge, that's breaking
a glass ceiling that is major.

00:24:00.651 --> 00:24:02.909
And so I think that that's important.

00:24:02.909 --> 00:24:06.611
On the other hand, we still
need to pass legislation

00:24:06.611 --> 00:24:08.451
for the Equal Rights Amendment.

00:24:08.451 --> 00:24:12.001
We need to amend the
constitution for pay equity,

00:24:12.001 --> 00:24:13.091
all of those things.

00:24:13.091 --> 00:24:16.831
So we still have a ways to
go, but it is very important

00:24:16.831 --> 00:24:18.651
just as you said, it's so important

00:24:18.651 --> 00:24:21.471
to acknowledge progress, because otherwise

00:24:21.471 --> 00:24:23.131
that adds two sentences.

00:24:23.131 --> 00:24:26.671
When people say, well, I
haven't seen major change,

00:24:26.671 --> 00:24:29.561
it's always important
to understand history,

00:24:29.561 --> 00:24:32.821
because if you know history
and understand history,

00:24:32.821 --> 00:24:35.751
then you can see how things do change.

00:24:35.751 --> 00:24:38.441
And that's why we need
everybody that's watching

00:24:38.441 --> 00:24:42.331
to make sure that they
one vote in 69 days,

00:24:42.331 --> 00:24:45.141
make sure you fill out your census form

00:24:45.141 --> 00:24:48.664
and then stay involved,
stay involved, we need you.

00:24:50.731 --> 00:24:51.581
And maybe the last thing,

00:24:51.581 --> 00:24:53.921
right before we went
live on this live stream,

00:24:53.921 --> 00:24:55.891
you and I were talking a little bit about,

00:24:55.891 --> 00:24:58.941
all of us were having to work
on video conferencing now.

00:24:58.941 --> 00:25:01.753
And I am curious about Congress itself,

00:25:01.753 --> 00:25:04.221
I read this, I read a lot
of science fiction books,

00:25:04.221 --> 00:25:05.791
a lot of them were behind me right now.

00:25:05.791 --> 00:25:09.491
But there's alternative models

00:25:09.491 --> 00:25:10.991
of representative democracies.

00:25:10.991 --> 00:25:13.371
Obviously ours is based on geography,

00:25:13.371 --> 00:25:15.241
because we didn't have even telephones

00:25:15.241 --> 00:25:18.191
when our government was architected.

00:25:18.191 --> 00:25:21.591
Do you think that there's gonna be things

00:25:21.591 --> 00:25:22.661
that come out of this crisis

00:25:22.661 --> 00:25:23.731
or even new ways of working

00:25:23.731 --> 00:25:26.341
that actually might alter
Congress as a whole.

00:25:26.341 --> 00:25:27.691
And I'm also curious, we touched on

00:25:27.691 --> 00:25:29.741
kind of the polarization a little bit.

00:25:29.741 --> 00:25:32.541
Is it as bad as it looks from the outside?

00:25:32.541 --> 00:25:34.574
And what is the cure for that?

00:25:35.621 --> 00:25:37.561
- Sure, well, first of all,

00:25:37.561 --> 00:25:40.861
I do think it's gonna alter
our way of functioning, period.

00:25:40.861 --> 00:25:42.031
And not just in Congress,

00:25:42.031 --> 00:25:45.011
I think all of us are
gonna be different now.

00:25:45.011 --> 00:25:47.591
And there's some good things about it,

00:25:47.591 --> 00:25:49.511
good things about it in terms of Zoom,

00:25:49.511 --> 00:25:54.147
in your ability to interact
with lots of people online.

00:25:54.147 --> 00:25:58.680
But in terms of the
polarization, I think that,

00:25:58.680 --> 00:26:03.191
I mean, I hate to say it this
way, but on the one hand,

00:26:03.191 --> 00:26:07.091
you see what you see from
us on TV is theater, okay?

00:26:07.091 --> 00:26:08.631
We don't hate each other.

00:26:08.631 --> 00:26:10.451
We actually do work together.

00:26:10.451 --> 00:26:12.401
We do disagree on some things,

00:26:12.401 --> 00:26:14.711
but there's lots of things we agree about.

00:26:14.711 --> 00:26:18.021
It's just that it tends that
that's not really newsworthy,

00:26:18.021 --> 00:26:21.221
so you don't hear when
we actually do things,

00:26:21.221 --> 00:26:23.031
and that needs to change.

00:26:23.031 --> 00:26:25.091
But I think the polarization,

00:26:25.091 --> 00:26:27.831
we need to have a less divisive leader.

00:26:27.831 --> 00:26:29.124
Let me leave it at that.

00:26:30.631 --> 00:26:32.981
- Okay, and you know, the last point,

00:26:32.981 --> 00:26:35.121
I mean, you mentioned what
we have to do in education.

00:26:35.121 --> 00:26:36.141
I completely agree with you,

00:26:36.141 --> 00:26:38.311
and I wrote an op-ed in
New York times last week,

00:26:38.311 --> 00:26:39.741
we can't let this education crisis

00:26:39.741 --> 00:26:41.561
turned to an education catastrophe,

00:26:41.561 --> 00:26:43.611
for all the reasons that you mentioned,

00:26:43.611 --> 00:26:46.761
where the kids who are
already not fully resourced

00:26:46.761 --> 00:26:49.481
are now the ones the most
likely to either not have access

00:26:49.481 --> 00:26:50.321
or when they do, they don't have

00:26:50.321 --> 00:26:52.390
the right supports, et cetera, et cetera.

00:26:52.390 --> 00:26:54.971
What could we do at Khan Academy?

00:26:54.971 --> 00:26:57.421
What advice do you have for us?

00:26:57.421 --> 00:27:00.551
And I also want advice
because a lot of folks

00:27:00.551 --> 00:27:01.384
have been telling us, you'll have

00:27:01.384 --> 00:27:02.851
to work more closely with government,

00:27:02.851 --> 00:27:04.651
in a lot of ways the work
that Khan Academy is doing

00:27:04.651 --> 00:27:06.634
is maybe somewhat a role of government.

00:27:07.601 --> 00:27:09.336
- Yes.
- How should we navigate this?

00:27:09.336 --> 00:27:11.451
(both laughing)

00:27:11.451 --> 00:27:13.661
- I think that's absolutely right.

00:27:13.661 --> 00:27:16.191
And you do play a tremendous role

00:27:16.191 --> 00:27:18.661
and it's a tremendous benefit to people.

00:27:18.661 --> 00:27:21.784
And maybe the way in terms of coordinating

00:27:21.784 --> 00:27:24.781
with government might be the case.

00:27:24.781 --> 00:27:28.841
And I think you becoming,
the Khan Academy becoming

00:27:28.841 --> 00:27:32.531
more well known, certainly
within the halls of Congress,

00:27:32.531 --> 00:27:34.531
there's people that know
and people that don't.

00:27:34.531 --> 00:27:37.171
And like I mentioned to you before,

00:27:37.171 --> 00:27:40.491
two of my staff members have
benefited by Khan Academy,

00:27:40.491 --> 00:27:42.531
you help them with calculus.

00:27:42.531 --> 00:27:44.461
And with me, I mentioned to you

00:27:44.461 --> 00:27:47.571
that I have used Khan Academy with adults

00:27:47.571 --> 00:27:50.471
who've experienced health crisis,

00:27:50.471 --> 00:27:52.261
like a heart issue, for example,

00:27:52.261 --> 00:27:54.631
and really didn't understand
how the heart worked.

00:27:54.631 --> 00:27:58.804
And so it's very helpful
in a variety of ways.

00:27:59.861 --> 00:28:01.247
- Now, I have to tell you,

00:28:01.247 --> 00:28:02.141
I think it was about
four or five years ago,

00:28:02.141 --> 00:28:03.441
I got separately invited

00:28:03.441 --> 00:28:05.961
to the House Republican Caucus retreat

00:28:05.961 --> 00:28:07.951
and the House democratic Caucus retreat,

00:28:07.951 --> 00:28:09.937
this was like a year apart.

00:28:09.937 --> 00:28:11.278
And when I presented, folks were like,

00:28:11.278 --> 00:28:12.911
"Wow, what kind of games to trying to do

00:28:12.911 --> 00:28:14.861
represents what our
party is trying to do."

00:28:14.861 --> 00:28:17.701
And both parties separately said this.

00:28:17.701 --> 00:28:19.986
So I feel like we might
be threading the needle

00:28:19.986 --> 00:28:23.901
on a bipartisan issue, so I
would love to work with you,

00:28:23.901 --> 00:28:27.201
to figure out how we can
especially reach the kids

00:28:27.201 --> 00:28:28.301
that need this most right now.

00:28:28.301 --> 00:28:30.571
And I 100% agree, a lot
of it could be free,

00:28:30.571 --> 00:28:32.211
it's just amplification.

00:28:32.211 --> 00:28:34.091
There's a lot of folks who
know about Khan Academy,

00:28:34.091 --> 00:28:36.081
but there's a lot of folks
who could know about it,

00:28:36.081 --> 00:28:38.311
who aren't benefiting fully just yet.

00:28:38.311 --> 00:28:40.141
- Exactly, and so you know what?

00:28:40.141 --> 00:28:42.751
I'm gonna go back and
tell our new caucus chair,

00:28:42.751 --> 00:28:45.175
you need to come back to
our retreat next year.

00:28:45.175 --> 00:28:46.538
(both laughing)

00:28:46.538 --> 00:28:49.101
- Sounds good, I'd
probably be less traveled,

00:28:49.101 --> 00:28:50.861
we'll probably be on Zoom.

00:28:50.861 --> 00:28:54.231
So hopefully we're through
through the crisis by then.

00:28:54.231 --> 00:28:58.001
Any last parting words of advice
for kids who are watching,

00:28:58.001 --> 00:28:59.911
who are just trying to absorb everything

00:28:59.911 --> 00:29:00.961
we just talked about?

00:29:01.961 --> 00:29:06.131
- Just that the most important
thing is to be healthy,

00:29:06.131 --> 00:29:10.651
to be safe, to abide by the
public health requirements,

00:29:10.651 --> 00:29:13.891
and make sure that your parents
fill out that census form,

00:29:13.891 --> 00:29:17.251
and just remember you are
never, never, never too young

00:29:17.251 --> 00:29:20.671
to get involved in politics
and community issues,

00:29:20.671 --> 00:29:22.094
there's always room for you.

00:29:23.951 --> 00:29:26.541
- Well, thank you so
much Representative Bass,

00:29:26.541 --> 00:29:28.271
this was an inspiring conversation.

00:29:28.271 --> 00:29:30.411
I say it every time, these
conversations go way faster

00:29:30.411 --> 00:29:32.351
than I expect, because
they're so interesting,

00:29:32.351 --> 00:29:34.261
but thank you for taking out the time,

00:29:34.261 --> 00:29:35.711
at a time that I know is very busy

00:29:35.711 --> 00:29:37.841
and a lot of important things going on.

00:29:37.841 --> 00:29:39.374
- My pleasure, thank you.

00:29:41.711 --> 00:29:43.764
- Well, thanks everyone
again for participating.

00:29:43.764 --> 00:29:45.871
I continue to be very impressed

00:29:45.871 --> 00:29:47.441
with everyone that we talk to,

00:29:47.441 --> 00:29:49.541
and especially Representative Bass,

00:29:49.541 --> 00:29:51.511
I find to be a very inspiring figure.

00:29:51.511 --> 00:29:54.631
But thanks for joining, hopefully
you got some good advice,

00:29:54.631 --> 00:29:55.671
hopefully we do figure out a way

00:29:55.671 --> 00:29:57.581
to work with Congress in some way

00:29:57.581 --> 00:29:59.421
to be able to support more folks

00:29:59.421 --> 00:30:02.201
as we go through this crisis
and then beyond the crisis.

00:30:02.201 --> 00:30:04.171
And I hope to see all of y'all tomorrow,

00:30:04.171 --> 00:30:07.551
where we're gonna be doing
another a Homeroom live stream,

00:30:07.551 --> 00:30:11.721
and this will just be one of
our classic Ask Sal Anythings.

00:30:11.721 --> 00:30:12.821
So I really look forward to that,

00:30:12.821 --> 00:30:15.841
that's always a fun thing for
me to just ask me anything.

00:30:15.841 --> 00:30:17.401
And so start thinking of your questions,

00:30:17.401 --> 00:30:20.821
and I'm literally happy to
answer a question about anything,

00:30:20.821 --> 00:30:23.051
I'm very hard to offend or surprise.

00:30:23.051 --> 00:30:25.004
So I will see y'all tomorrow.

00:30:26.281 --> 00:30:28.864
(upbeat music)

